---
/**
 * Starlight's `<head>`, plus the client-side router.
 *
 * `Head` is the supported injection point for `<ClientRouter />` — Starlight
 * renders the whole `<html>` element from `Page.astro`, so there is nowhere
 * else in the theme a document-level component can be added.
 *
 * Why the router is here at all: the tab strip has to survive navigation. It is
 * a persisted island, and Astro's swap moves the live DOM node into the new
 * document rather than rebuilding it, so the tab set, an open menu and focus all
 * outlive a page change. Without the router every navigation would tear the
 * strip down and mount a fresh one — which is not a tab strip, it is a row of
 * buttons that resets whenever you use it. It is also the only arrangement in
 * which an anchored popover (the appearance panel, and the regex builder and
 * appearance editors that mount beside it) can stay open across a navigation
 * instead of being destroyed mid-interaction.
 *
 * What this costs, stated plainly rather than discovered later:
 *  - Every module `<script>` in the tree now runs once for the whole session,
 *    not once per page. Astro keys executed scripts by src/text and skips the
 *    ones it has already run, so any component script doing one-time DOM setup
 *    must re-bind on `astro:page-load` or it is dead from the second page on.
 *    All four of this site's scripts were converted for that reason.
 *  - The swap replaces `<html>`'s attributes wholesale, taking `data-theme` and
 *    the inline `--m3-*` tokens with them. `installAppearanceRuntime` repaints
 *    in `astro:after-swap`; see the comment there.
 *  - Starlight does not ship or bless view-transition support, so the
 *    interaction with its sidebar persister is verified rather than guaranteed
 *    and is worth re-checking at each Starlight upgrade.
 *
 * Starlight's own `Head` takes no slot, so nothing is forwarded into it.
 */
import Default from '@astrojs/starlight/components/Head.astro';
import { ClientRouter } from 'astro:transitions';
---

<Default />
<ClientRouter />
